  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Enhancing Disaster Risk Management (DRM) in Libya</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Libya is experiencing the impact of climate change, which manifests in various forms and acts as a risk multiplier, intersecting with political, social, economic, and demographic factors. The country is registering an increasing trend in the frequency and intensity of disasters such as desertification, floods, sandstorms, and land/mudslides. A comprehensive research report on the interaction between climate change and environmental degradation, water scarcity, mobility and community cohesion dynamics in Libya is underway. The severity and frequency of extreme weather events increasingly affect vulnerable populations, including migrants and internally displaced population. National authorities as well as communities themselves remain unequipped in terms of preparedness and response, and often authorities lack capacity while the resilience of communities affected by disasters /at risk of being affected remains limited. Therefore, this project aims to contribute to strengthening the resilience of disaster-affected communities / communities at risk of being affected, and reinforcing national response to mitigate the impact of natural disasters in Libya. Through this project, IOM intends to build the capacity of Libyan authorities and provide support to identify and document the related deficiencies, as well as build an evidence baseline to guide further initiatives.</narrative>
  </description>
